  "strongest_justified_minimal_conditional_claim": "Under the original explicitly trusted OS/POSIX/loader/native-hashing and authenticated closed-startup/closed-domain premises, the named later successful OS operations may be interpreted with their genuine Linux semantics when actually performed by the admitted original parent. This reuses provider integrity already required by the source; it does not introduce a new global OS-integrity axiom merely because the initial checker has returned. The initial receipt itself remains a bounded fresh corroboration. Complete compiler-input origin/witness coverage, relevant K dispositions, private compiler ABI/callee interpretations, actual interval ownership/installed policy, original error/resource conditions and current parent/runtime admission require their own stated evidence.",
  "supported_temporal_inference": "Existing genuine-OS/POSIX provider semantics are relied upon after bootstrap through launch, observation, cleanup, terminal checks, custody and parent teardown. There is no source basis for automatic universal expiration at bootstrap. This is an interpretation of the explicit premise and actual use sites, not an independently proved perpetual integrity guarantee."
